<pdd-reason>The pitch intake and public board reads. Accepts a pitch into Redis without ever persisting it, and serves the board from Postgres.</pdd-reason>

<pdd-interface>
{"type":"module","module":{"functions":[
  {"name":"router","signature":"router: APIRouter","returns":"prefix /api/features, tag features"},
  {"name":"create_pitch","signature":"POST /api/features","returns":"202 {feature_id, state}"},
  {"name":"list_features","signature":"GET /api/features","returns":"200 paged board"},
  {"name":"list_my_pitches","signature":"GET /api/features/mine","returns":"200 {pending, features}"},
  {"name":"get_feature","signature":"GET /api/features/{feature_id}","returns":"200 Feature | 404"}
]}}
</pdd-interface>

<pdd-dependency>prompts/backend/deps_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/shared/constants_python.prompt</pdd-dependency>

Implement `backend/routes/features.py`. Clients, settings, and caller identity all arrive through
`Depends` on `backend.deps` — this module constructs nothing.

<responsibility>
Accept a pitch onto the intake queue, and read the public board.
</responsibility>

<non_responsibilities>
This module DOES NOT screen, dedup, classify, or publish a pitch — the orchestrator owns all four.
This module DOES NOT write to `feature_requests`; only the orchestrator creates a board row.
This module DOES NOT call an LLM, ever.
</non_responsibilities>

<vocabulary>
Valid: of a pitch, one whose cleaned title and description both fall inside the bounds in the frozen request schema and contain no markup. Validity is decided before a coin is spent, and the backend's answer overrides the client's.
**Pitch Coin** — one unit of an author's daily submission allowance, counted at `rate:pitch:{author_id}`.
**Board row** — a row in `feature_requests`. A pitch becomes one only after it survives screening.
</vocabulary>

<capabilities>
- MAY `INCR`/`EXPIRE` the rate key, `LPUSH` to `feature_intake`, and `SET` a pending-pitch record.
- MAY `SCAN` and read the **calling author's own** pending-pitch records.
- MAY read `feature_requests`.
- MUST NOT write, update, or delete any Postgres row.
- MUST NOT call an LLM or any orchestrator process.
</capabilities>

## POST /api/features (US-01)

Body `{title, description}`; `title` 1–60 chars, `description` 30–300 (`openapi.yaml`).

This endpoint is the deterministic gate. Everything it can decide without a model — shape, length,
invisible characters, embedded markup — it decides here, so the screener only ever spends a model
call on text that is already well-formed.
Requires an identified caller via `get_current_user_id`.

Governed by R1–R13 and R22–R27 in the contract rules below.

## GET /api/features (US-05) and GET /api/features/{feature_id}

Anonymous — `get_optional_user_id`, never `get_current_user_id`. R8–R13 govern these two routes.

## GET /api/features/mine (US-06)

The author's private view. Requires an identified caller via `get_current_user_id`. Two
independent reads — Redis for what is still in screening, Postgres for what became a board row —
composed only in the response.

Governed by R14–R21 in the contract rules below.

In the MVP a pending record's `state` is always `screening`. `openapi.yaml` also permits
`rejected` and `merged` with a reason and a canonical-feature link; nothing writes them yet, so
the enum values are reserved rather than dead. A pitch that screening drops is not marked — its
record simply expires, which is the deliberate scope decision recorded in the pdd block below.

Every error response is the frozen envelope `{"error": {"code", "message"}}` — never FastAPI's
default `{"detail"}`. `code` is machine-readable (`out_of_coins`, `validation_failed`);
`message` is copy the frontend may display verbatim.

`author_handle` is nullable and this route never populates it: there is no accounts table in the
MVP (`schema.sql`), so the column stays null until one exists. Do not invent a value.

<contract_rules>
R1 (MUST): Return `202` with exactly `{"feature_id": <uuid>, "state": "screening"}` — the frozen response shape in `openapi.yaml`, where `state` is the constant `screening`, not a status enum.
R2 (MUST): Write the `pending_pitch:{author_id}:{feature_id}` record **before** the `LPUSH` to `feature_intake`. The orchestrator can screen and publish a result within milliseconds; if the record does not exist yet the outcome write silently no-ops and the author's pitch vanishes from their own tray (US-06).
R3 (MUST NOT): Write pitch content to Postgres on any path. Unscreened content lives only in Redis, under a TTL.
R4 (MUST): On exhausted coins return `429` with `{"error": {"code": "out_of_coins", "message": …}, "resets_at": <ISO-8601>}` — both fields required by `openapi.yaml`; `resets_at` drives the frontend countdown, so it MUST be a real timestamp, never null.
R5 (MUST): Count coins per author per UTC calendar day, so `resets_at` is the next UTC midnight and the "try again tomorrow" copy is true.
R6 (MUST NOT): Return `author_id` in any response body.
R7 (MUST NOT): Write pitch title or description into a log line or an error message.
R8 (MUST): Serve reads only from `feature_requests`, so a pitch still in screening and a rejected pitch are both invisible here. This is what keeps unscreened content off the public board (US-01).
R9 (MUST): Take a required `view` of `pipeline | shipped | holding | vault`, an optional `sort` of `top` (default, `upvotes` desc) or `new` (`created_at` desc), an optional `limit` (default 30, max 100), and an optional opaque `cursor`. These names and values are frozen in `openapi.yaml` — `newest`, `page`, or `page_size` are all wrong.
R10 (MUST): Paginate by **keyset** on the sort column, returning an opaque `cursor` a caller passes back. Offset pagination MUST NOT be used: the board reorders live as votes land, so an offset silently skips or repeats rows between pages.
R11 (MUST): Return `{"features": [...]}` plus the cursor. A total count is not in the frozen response and MUST NOT be added — counting every matching row on each board load is the expensive query on the hottest path.
R12 (MUST): Reject an unknown `status` value with `400`, and treat `status` as a CSV of canonical enum values valid only in the `pipeline` view.
R13 (MUST): Return `404` when the id matches no row.
R14 (MUST): Return `{"pending": [...], "features": [...]}`, both keys always present and both arrays possibly empty. The frozen shape in `openapi.yaml` has no total, no cursor, and no outcome summary.
R15 (MUST): Scope the Redis scan to the literal prefix `pending_pitch:{caller_id}:*`. Scanning all pending keys and filtering by author in Python MUST NOT be used — it walks every author's unscreened pitches through this process to answer one author's question, and one bad filter leaks them.
R16 (MUST): Use `SCAN`, never `KEYS`. `KEYS` blocks the single-threaded Redis server for the whole keyspace, and this endpoint is polled by every author with a pitch in flight.
R17 (MUST): Filter out any pending entry whose `feature_id` already appears in the `features` array. A pitch that clears screening gets a board row while its Redis record is still inside its TTL, so without this the author sees the same pitch twice — once as "screening", once as `VOTING` — and the two disagree. This is a reconciliation of two already-fetched result sets, not a join: the stores are still queried independently and MUST NOT be joined, cached together, or indexed against each other.
R18 (MUST): Read the Postgres side filtered by `author_id`, and return each row's real `status` so `VOTING`, `IN_SPRINT`, and `COMPILED` are distinguishable — those are three of the six outcomes US-06 asks for.
R19 (MUST NOT): Delete, rewrite, or extend a pending record here. This route is read-only over Redis: the TTL set at pitch time is the sole mechanism that clears a pending entry, whether the pitch passed, was rejected, or was merged.
R20 (MUST): Declare this route **before** `GET /api/features/{feature_id}` in the module. FastAPI matches in declaration order, so a `{feature_id}` route declared first swallows the literal `mine` and answers `404` for a valid request. Both routes living in this one module is what keeps that ordering a visible, local property.
R21 (MUST NOT): Accept an author id from the query string, body, or a header. The caller can only ever read their own pitches, and the id comes from `get_current_user_id` alone.
R22 (MUST): Strip Unicode control characters from `title` and `description` before any other check — category `Cc`/`Cf`, plus zero-width and bidirectional-override codepoints. Keep `\n` and `\t` in `description` only; `title` is single-line, so strip those there too. They are invisible, usually arrive by paste, and would otherwise reach the queue, the model, and the board, where they can hide text or break rendering.
R23 (MUST): Reject — never sanitise — a `title` or `description` containing raw HTML or script markup, with `400` and `error.code` `validation_failed`. Stripping tags silently changes what the author wrote and can still leave a working payload; refusing tells them what to fix. Detect a tag-like construct (`<tag …>`, `</tag>`, `<script`, and HTML entity escapes for the same), not merely a bare `<` or `>`, so comparisons like `width < 300px` still pass.
R24 (MUST): Apply the length bounds to the **cleaned** text, not the raw body. A description padded to 30 characters with zero-width spaces is not 30 characters of content, and validating the raw string would let it through.
R25 (MUST): Enqueue and store exactly the cleaned text. The string that passed validation is the one written to the pending record and to the intake payload, so the author's tray, the screener, and the board can never show three different versions of one pitch.
R26 (MUST): Run R22–R24 **before** the Pitch Coin is checked or spent. A submission the server refused outright never got a screening attempt, so charging for it would burn an author's daily allowance on a typo.
R27 (MUST NOT): Echo the offending text back in the validation message. Name the field and the rule that failed — the copy is displayed verbatim to the author (`openapi.yaml` ErrorBody), and reflecting unscreened input into a response is how a rejected payload reaches a browser.
R28 (MUST): Write the intake envelope with exactly these five keys — `feature_id`, `author_id`, `title`, `description`, `submitted_at` — and no others. This is a contract between two processes that never import each other: the orchestrator validates the key set exactly and drops anything else as malformed, so adding or removing one here strands every pitch in the queue with no error visible from this side.
R29 (MUST): Return **root rows only** from `GET /api/features` — rows whose `parent_id` is null. A split child is part of its parent's idea, so listing it top-level turns one pitch into three unrelated cards and hides the parent that explains them.
R30 (MUST): Embed a `SPLIT` parent's children in its `children` array, ordered oldest-first, each a full `Feature` object. `openapi.yaml` declares the field as "Present on SPLIT parents", and the board renders the family as one card with its parts inside — which is the only presentation where voting on a part makes sense.
R31 (MUST): Keep returning a child directly from `GET /api/features/{feature_id}` and from `/api/features/mine` when it is the row asked for. Root-only is a rule about *lists*; an author who split a pitch must still find its children in their own tray (US-06).
R32 (MUST): Fetch children with one query for the whole page, not one per parent. A page of 30 split parents would otherwise issue 31 round-trips on the hottest read in the product.
R33 (MUST): Always include `children` as a list on every feature in a list response — an empty list when the row has no children, never null and never absent. The frontend maps over it directly, so an omitted key forces every card to guard a field the schema already describes as a list.

R34 (MUST): Return **every** field of the `Feature` schema on every feature in every response, embedded children included: `id, title, description, status, upvotes, author_handle, parent_id, split_depth, unlock_threshold, extends_id, extends_title, postpone_count, ai_explanation, merge_count, shipped_version, shipped_at, viewer_has_voted, children, created_at, updated_at`. A narrower projection silently disables whole features that are already built and tested against fixtures: `ai_explanation` missing empties the Holding Pattern's "Why the pause" panel, which is the only account the community ever gets of why their feature was held; `merge_count` and `extends_title` missing erase the visible evidence that dedup merged pitches rather than losing them; `unlock_threshold` missing blanks every unlock-tree progress bar. This has already shipped once — the projection listed ten of the twenty fields and no test noticed, because the component tests use fixtures rather than the live endpoint.
R35 (MUST NOT): Widen that projection to include `author_id`. R6 still holds: the full Feature schema is the target, and `author_id` is not in it.
R36 (MUST): Resolve `shipped_version` and `shipped_at` from the `feature_shipped_meta` view rather than from `feature_requests`, which has no such columns. The view's own columns are `feature_id, version, preview_url, deployed_at` — **`version` populates `shipped_version` and `deployed_at` populates `shipped_at`**. Selecting the wire names straight from the view raises `42703: column does not exist`; that is not a hypothetical, it is what a previous generation did. They are null for anything that has not shipped.
R37 (MUST): Compute `viewer_has_voted` per row from `feature_votes` for the identified viewer, in **one** query for the whole page rather than one per row, and return `false` for an anonymous reader rather than null. It is what stops a returning visitor being invited to click a control that will answer `409`; defaulting it to false for everyone makes the board lie to exactly the people who already participated.
R39 (MUST): Map the four views to statuses exactly: `pipeline` → `VOTING, CONSOLIDATING, IN_SPRINT, SPLIT`; `shipped` → `COMPILED`; `holding` → `POSTPONED_CONFLICT`; `vault` → `ARCHIVED`. A `SPLIT` parent belongs on the **pipeline**: its children are open for voting and the unlock tree is a pipeline interaction. Filing it under holding tells the community their idea was blocked when in fact it was broken into parts they can vote for — the opposite message. This mapping has already drifted once during a regeneration, unpinned, and no rule caught it.
R38 (MUST): Apply R34, R36, and R37 to `GET /api/features/{feature_id}` and to `/api/features/mine` as well. One shape means one renderer — a card that carries its explanation on the board but loses it in the author's own tray is the kind of inconsistency that gets fixed by adding a second code path.
</contract_rules>

<pdd>
Scope decision for the MVP demo, deliberately taken: **screening outcomes are not reported.**
A pending record is written at pitch time with a TTL and is never updated again. If the pitch
passes it becomes a board row and R17 hides the stale pending twin; if it is rejected or merged
the record just expires. That descopes two of US-06's acceptance criteria — "rejected (with
reason category)" and "merged pitches link to the canonical feature" — in exchange for deleting
the entire outcome-notification path (the `screening_results` subscriber, the record mutation,
and the TTL reset) from the MVP.

The cost is time-boxed by exactly one dial: `PENDING_PITCH_TTL_SECONDS`, set to 900 (15 minutes).
Until it expires, a rejected pitch reads "screening" to its author — so the value is chosen to be
long enough that a slow screening pass never erases a pitch that is still being worked on, and
short enough that "still screening" stays plausible to the person reading it. Real screening
completes in seconds to minutes, so 15 minutes is roughly an order of magnitude of headroom.

Raising it back toward hours re-opens the same wrong answer for that much longer; lowering it
below a few minutes risks clearing a pending entry while its pitch is genuinely still in the
queue, which reads to the author as the pitch being thrown away.
</pdd>

